WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.620
The series may be hurtling toward the finish
line, but Fast X still sets aside some time

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.500
to nod towards its roots.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:10.660
From mixing up the formula to some very familiar
quotes, here are some of the best callbacks

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:11.660
in Fast X.

00:00:12.098 --> 00:00:15.710
“Fast X” spends a lot of time calling
back to the events of “Fast Five.”

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:19.490
Chief among them is Dante Reyes is the son
of Hernan Reyes.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:23.640
Appropriately, the movie opens with a redux
of the “Fast Five” climax, in which Dom

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.680
and Brian steal the Reyes’ safe.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.360
This time, though, Dante is there.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.080
If you haven’t seen the fifth film in a
while, you might not notice just how close

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.320
“Fast X” slots him into the action.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:36.610
The car he drives during the bridge battle
— the one with the minigun that pops out

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:39.450
of the top — is pulled straight from the
original scene.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.890
This creates a fun time loop moment for anyone
who goes back to “Fast Five” in the future.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:48.637
The later parts of “Fast X” also make
reference to Dante’s retconned “Fast Five” cameo.

00:00:48.637 --> 00:00:52.980
He has a base of operations in the abandoned
police station where Dom and Brian stole the

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:58.500
safe, and he ambushes Dom on the very same
bridge, thus reversing his previous defeat there.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.395
Typically, “Fast and Furious” movies end
with a family barbecue scene.

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:05.518
“Fast X” changes things up by starting
with one instead.

00:01:05.518 --> 00:01:09.610
This is an early clue that things aren’t
going to go well in the third act.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:13.159
Because the movie ends on a huge cliffhanger,
with the lives of multiple major characters

00:01:13.159 --> 00:01:16.820
hanging in the balance, there’s no room
for a feel-good finale.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:19.820
The opening cookout is still full of fun little
details, though.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:23.820
The whole gang can be seen sipping on Coronas,
which have been the unofficial official beer

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.659
of “Fast and Furious” since the beginning.

00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:28.525
"You can have any brew you want.

00:01:28.525 --> 00:01:30.340
As long as it's a corona."

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.220
And Roman once again tries to sneak some food
before the blessing has been said, just as

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.840
he has many times before.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.180
There’s also a cute moment between Little
Brian and Letty.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.130
When Dom walks into the barbecue with his
son after their driving lesson, Brian gives

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:45.020
Letty a hug and calls her “Mom.”

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.960
Letty may not actually be Brian’s blood
mother, but she’s raised him as her own

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.650
since he was incredibly young.

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:54.440
It’s nice to see how their relationship
has blossomed, and the little glimpse of tenderness

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.549
from Letty is a perfect character moment.

00:01:57.202 --> 00:02:00.659
During the cookout scene at the beginning
of “Fast X” Dom and Han share a moment

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:02.530
of reflection on their thieving origins.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:06.950
As Han says, “We’ve come a long way from
flipping fuel trucks in the D.R.”

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:10.369
This is a direct reference to the beginning
of “Fast &amp; Furious,” the fourth film in

00:02:10.369 --> 00:02:15.530
the franchise, in which Dom, Han, Letty, Leo
and Santos pulled various small-time heists

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:17.880
in the Dominican Republic to make ends meet.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:22.019
Their stint there culminated in a fuel truck
robbery gone horribly wrong -- the first big

00:02:22.019 --> 00:02:24.120
action set piece of “Fast &amp; Furious.”

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:28.810
As they reminisce, Han offers a toast, saying,
“Los Bandoleros for life.”

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:32.750
“Los Bandoleros” is the name of the short
film prequel to “Fast &amp; Furious,” which

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:33.750
details how the D.R.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.180
crew was first assembled.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:39.070
The Dominican Republic stories are technically
Han’s earliest entry in the “Fast and

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:42.940
Furious” timeline, not counting Justin Lin’s
film “Better Luck Tomorrow,” which serves

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.709
as an unofficial prequel of sorts.

00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:48.560
And of course, Vin Diesel himself actually
directed “Los Bandoleros,” making the

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.036
reference extra special for Dom as well.

00:02:51.407 --> 00:02:55.550
Right after his “Los Bandoleros” reminiscing
with Han, Dom takes a moment to glance around

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:59.530
his garage and take note of the many family
photos he’s tacked up on the walls.

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:03.959
The camera lingers on several of him and Brian
together, and as it does, the piano refrain

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:06.980
of “See You Again” blends in with the
original score.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.739
The Wiz Khalifa and Charlie Puth anthem was
written to commemorate the late Paul Walker,

00:03:10.739 --> 00:03:15.770
and it became a massive hit in its own right
after debuting at the emotional end of “Furious 7.”

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:20.300
The “Fast” franchise has always handled
Walker’s legacy with tact and respect, choosing

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:24.959
to leave him out of the story rather than
recast the role or bring him back with CGI.

00:03:24.959 --> 00:03:28.750
Even in a movie like “Fast X,” where Brian’s
absence doesn’t really affect the story,

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:33.760
it’s nice to see Diesel and the crew still
take a moment to pay homage to his life and career.

00:03:33.898 --> 00:03:37.680
After the opening barbecue, Dom gets vulnerable
about his fears.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.119
He admits being scared of losing Brian or
Letty, and she tells him that won’t happen “again.”

00:03:42.119 --> 00:03:46.100
This is a two-for-one reference, calling back
to both Letty’s (almost) death in “Fast

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:50.200
&amp; Furious” and the time Brian was kidnapped
by Cipher in “The Fate of the Furious.”

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:54.200
In Letty’s case, she lost her memory and
was out of the picture for two whole movies,

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:58.750
only getting brought back into the fold by
a persistent Dom in “Fast &amp; Furious 6.”

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:02.970
Brian was likely too young during his captivity
to remember much of it in detail, but we see

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:06.250
later in “Fast X” how the trauma of the
experience still affects him.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:11.459
Later on in the movie, we get another nod
to Letty’s brief time working for Owen Shaw.

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:15.189
When Tess visits her in prison, she points
out a scar on Letty’s arm that she got the

00:04:15.189 --> 00:04:18.739
night she first met Dom, thereby proving that
he sent her.

00:04:18.739 --> 00:04:22.979
This is the exact same scar and story that
Dom uses to convince Letty of their history

00:04:22.979 --> 00:04:24.729
in “Fast &amp; Furious 6.”

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.000
In any “Fast and Furious” movie there
are going to be some standout cars.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.970
Sometimes the vehicles driven by the crew
are simply chosen because they look neat,

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:34.690
but sometimes they have a deeper meaning.

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:39.490
To start, Cipher enters the film behind the
wheel of a Delorean Alpha 5 -- a yet-to-be-released

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:44.720
limited edition homage to the Delorean Motor
Company’s heyday with the iconic DMC-12.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.400
The back-to-the-futuristic look of the car
is fitting for Cipher’s techno-terrorist

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.900
aesthetic, and since the car isn’t out yet,
it’s safe to assume that she stole one.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:57.240
During the big action sequence in Rome, Han
drives a boxy Alpha Romeo with an orange and

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:58.389
black color scheme.

00:04:58.389 --> 00:05:03.661
It’s the exact same coloring that his Mazda
RX-7 has in “The Fast and the Furious: Tokyo Drift.”

00:05:03.661 --> 00:05:08.490
And of course, Jakob gets to drive his old
blue 5.0 “Fox Body” Mustang, which was

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:12.190
first seen during the flashback scenes in
“F9: The Fast Saga.”

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:16.490
Jakob references his drag race with Dom while
talking to Little Brian, and he also mentions

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:19.289
how cool it was to have a 5.0 back in the
day.

00:05:19.289 --> 00:05:23.190
Clearly, Brian isn’t all that impressed
by Mustangs, as his project car shown earlier

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:26.090
in the movie is actually a Chevy SS.

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:29.910
Given that his dad loves Dodge and his uncle
loves Ford, it feels right that the kid goes

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:32.930
with a third major American car manufacturer.

00:05:33.564 --> 00:05:37.830
The first big action set piece in “Fast
X” takes place in Rome, during a mission

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:39.680
led by, appropriately, Roman.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:45.720
He, Han, Tej, and Ramsey are tricked by Dante
into stealing a bomb, believing that the operation

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:47.960
has been commissioned by the Agency.

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:51.699
Through it all, Roman constantly asserts his
own abilities as a leader and takes offense

00:05:51.699 --> 00:05:53.580
every time the others doubt his chops.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:58.010
While not necessarily an intentional reference,
this little arc for Roman is interesting given

00:05:58.010 --> 00:05:59.570
his history in the saga.

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:03.580
He first appeared in “2 Fast 2 Furious”
as the replacement for Vin Diesel, who opted

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:05.970
to film “The Chronicles of Riddick” instead.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:09.389
In another timeline, that could have led to
Tyrese Gibson being one of the two leading

00:06:09.389 --> 00:06:13.610
co-stars for the series and Roman quite literally
being the leader of the crew.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:17.230
But instead, the character became comic relief
under Dom’s shadow.

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:20.520
For fans of the “2 Fast” era, it’s fun
to see Roman getting a bit of development

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:21.979
on the leadership end.

00:06:21.979 --> 00:06:25.620
Gibson is still more than capable of stealing
scenes on his own, and he gets some solid

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:27.470
material in “Fast X.”

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:31.340
There is a more specific “2 Fast” reference
later when Roman reveals that he keeps cash

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:33.680
strapped to himself under his clothes.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:36.220
He pulls the exact same move at the end of
the second film.

00:06:36.961 --> 00:06:41.550
The Rome sequence of “Fast X” is action-packed,
but it also includes a number of smaller references

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:43.690
to the earlier “Fast and Furious” films.

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:47.440
As part of the caper, Ramsey winds up behind
the wheel of a big truck carrying a piece

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.479
of dangerous tech -- in this case, a giant
bomb.

00:06:50.479 --> 00:06:54.250
This seems like a clear nod to the Edinburgh
sequence in “F9: The Fast Saga,” in which

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:57.419
she was forced to drive a truck carrying a
super-magnet.

00:06:57.419 --> 00:07:01.440
Letty also chooses to gear up on a motorcycle
instead of in a regular car, which is another

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.050
nod to “F9.”

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:06.410
During the minefield sequence of that film,
she’s shown to be a master both behind the

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:08.630
wheel and behind the handlebars.

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:12.570
And as is only right when putting Han inside
an orange and black sports car, he does a

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:15.509
bit of drifting for the camera, harkening
back to his time in Tokyo.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:20.350
More than any other piece of the “Fast and
Furious” story, “Fast X” seems fixated

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:22.800
on the vault heist from the end of “Fast
Five.”

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.060
It opens by reliving the chase through Dante’s
eyes, it returns to the very same police station

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:31.210
and bridge later on, and it pays homage to
the scene on several other occasions.

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:34.170
The bomb chase in Rome is an obvious nod to
the safe robbery.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:38.583
Dante himself acknowledges this, claiming
to have set it up as an homage to his family’s downfall.

00:07:38.583 --> 00:07:42.950
What he doesn’t mention is that Dom drives
a Dodge Charger Hellcat during the Rome sequence

00:07:42.950 --> 00:07:47.750
— a variation of the exact same cars that
he and Brian use to pull the vault in “Fast

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:48.750
Five.”

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:51.940
In the wake of the bomb’s explosion, Tess
also alludes to the vault heist after Dom

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:55.090
chastises her for carrying God’s Eye on
her person.

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:58.639
She says, “People pull vaults out of walls,
so I think it’s safer with me.”

00:07:58.639 --> 00:08:02.300
As if that weren’t enough, “Fast X”
drops one more vault chase reference during

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:03.970
its own fiery climax.

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:08.520
As he chases down Dante and Little Brian,
Dom is attacked by two helicopters that fire

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:11.819
grappling hooks into his car and try to lift
him off the road.

00:08:11.819 --> 00:08:15.889
His car gets out just enough horsepower to
yank both choppers down in flames, and he

00:08:15.889 --> 00:08:20.431
uses them both as makeshift melee weapons
just like he once did with the vault.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:24.450
If there’s one thing “Fast and Furious”
movies love to do, it’s quote the original.

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:29.310
And a little ways into the second act of “Fast
X,” Little Brian does just that.

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:33.957
After he beats his aunt Mia in a round of
“Hot Wheels Unleashed,” she jokingly chastises him.

00:08:33.957 --> 00:08:37.230
Brian responds with his own version of this
famous quote:

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:40.514
“It don't matter if you win by an inch or
a mile.

00:08:40.514 --> 00:08:42.053
Winning’s winning.”

00:08:42.053 --> 00:08:46.124
It isn’t even the first time Brian references
that particular scene from the first movie.

00:08:46.124 --> 00:08:49.339
After his “driving lesson” with Dom at
the beginning of the film, the little guy

00:08:49.339 --> 00:08:52.170
is frustrated that he can’t quite get things
right.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:56.230
“You almost had it,” his dad says, but
Brian responds “I didn’t have it.”

00:08:56.230 --> 00:09:00.330
This is yet another part of that famous scene
between Brian O’Conner and Dom.

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:03.505
After losing their first street race against
each other, Brian tells Dom,

00:09:03.505 --> 00:09:04.505
"Dude, I almost had you.”

00:09:04.677 --> 00:09:09.375
“Almost had me?

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.842
You never had me.

00:09:11.842 --> 00:09:13.253
You never had your car.”

00:09:13.607 --> 00:09:17.360
Even when the Vatican is being bombed and
armies of cyber-commandos are at the door,

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.601
“Fast and Furious” still makes time for
some good ol’ street racing.

00:09:20.601 --> 00:09:21.861
"Let's race."

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:26.180
In “Fast X,” Dom travels back to Rio to
confront Dante Reyes, and they end up meeting

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:29.480
at a racing event very similar to the one
seen in “Fast Five.”

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:33.980
In fact, one of the local characters in that
film, Diogo, returns and backs Dom up when

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:38.329
Dante appears, mirroring the face-off between
Hobbs and Dom in “Fast Five.”

00:09:38.329 --> 00:09:40.930
We get another little callback during the
race itself.

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:46.435
Diogo gets a bit too antsy and pops his nos
early, making him a target in Dante’s twisted game.

00:09:46.521 --> 00:09:50.790
The “too soon” nitrous moment is a classic
of the franchise, going all the way back to

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:52.351
Dom and Brian’s first drag.

00:09:52.351 --> 00:09:54.579
This time, though, the mistake is extra deadly.

00:09:54.579 --> 00:09:55.640
A cop and a street racer

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:59.920
As it turns out, Isabel is actually the sister
of Elena Neves, the late mother of Little

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:02.870
Brian who was killed by Cipher in “The Fate
of the Furious.”

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:06.800
Dom saves her from Dante’s bomb and takes
her back to Elena’s old apartment, where

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:08.190
Isabel now lives.

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:12.279
She confesses to him how much guilt she used
to feel over Elena dying instead of her, since

00:10:12.279 --> 00:10:16.290
her sister was always the “good” one and
Isabel saw herself as “bad.”

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:20.190
In response, Dom explains how much common
ground there can be between “a cop and a

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:23.480
street racer” when they go down “the same
road” long enough.

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:27.300
Clearly, this is a reference to his relationship
with Brian, which began when the latter was

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:30.790
investigating him for DVD player robberies
while undercover.

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:35.019
Over time, Brian abandoned his law enforcement
career to become a full-time crook and brother

00:10:35.019 --> 00:10:36.019
to Dom.

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:40.140
One of the best storylines in “Fast X”
is the uncle-nephew road trip that Jakob goes

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:41.140
on with Little Brian.

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:45.010
It’s a fun, lighthearted distraction from
the heavy action of the film, and it yields

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:48.220
one particularly poignant moment between the
two characters.

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:50.380
"Holy shi-"
"Cannon cars."

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:51.620
No, not that one.

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:55.389
While on the trip, Brian admits that he doesn’t
like planes, prompting his uncle to share

00:10:55.389 --> 00:10:59.459
how he was once terrified of cars after the
accident that killed his father.

00:10:59.459 --> 00:11:03.720
Of course, the reason Little B is so frightened
of planes is he was imprisoned on one as a baby.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:08.000
In a movie where Cipher becomes a begrudging
ally, this little moment reminds us of just

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:09.790
how vile she truly is.

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:13.290
She forever changed the course of Brian’s
life by taking his mother’s in “The Fate

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:16.810
of the Furious,” and though she and Letty
have set a temporary truce, she’ll have

00:11:16.810 --> 00:11:18.529
to answer for her crimes eventually.

00:11:19.529 --> 00:11:23.550
The climax chase sequence of “Fast X”
has a few notable nods to the series past.

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:27.690
For instance, when Dom enters the fray, he
air-drops out of the back of a cargo plan.

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:31.990
Although the “Fast X” drop is much lower,
it might remind viewers of the multi-car air-drop

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:34.530
in “Furious 7,” which actually involved
parachutes.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:39.279
At this point, Dominic Toretto is a true veteran
of driving muscle cars out of airplanes.

00:11:39.279 --> 00:11:43.459
Later, during the standoff atop the dam, Dom
is forced to drive straight down the face

00:11:43.459 --> 00:11:47.170
of the structure in order to save Brian, who’s
riding in the passenger seat.

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:51.079
He barely outruns the explosion caused by
Dante, thanks in large part to him hitting

00:11:51.079 --> 00:11:53.380
the nitrous at the exact right moment.

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:57.601
This triggers a CGI nos-infusion shot that
harkens back to the series’ roots.

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:00.220
“Fast X” ends with two big twists.

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:04.160
First, Giselle appears in the nuclear submarine
from “The Fate of The Furious” to rescue

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:05.690
Letty and Cipher from Antarctica.

00:12:05.690 --> 00:12:09.290
After that, there’s a mid-credits scene
in which Dwayne Johnson’s Luke Hobbs makes

00:12:09.290 --> 00:12:11.730
his triumphant return to the “Fast” saga.

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:14.060
Both reveals are shocking, but for different
reasons.

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:17.839
Hobbs’ appearance is surprising because
of behind-the-scenes issues, while Giselle’s

00:12:17.839 --> 00:12:20.810
is surprising because, well, she died.

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:24.340
Even so, her return is foreshadowed by a couple
different moments throughout the film.

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:28.300
During the opening cookout, Tej and Roman
help Han make a dating profile, but his heart

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:29.690
clearly isn’t in it.

00:12:29.690 --> 00:12:33.300
Later, while speaking with Tess, Aimes mentions
that the Toretto crew was involved in the

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:35.149
heist of a nuclear submarine.

00:12:35.149 --> 00:12:38.540
Letting the audience know that the vessel
was never recovered paves the way for it to

